<header class="topbar"><div class="wrap"><a class="wordmark" href="../"><span class="mark"></span>Critical Materials Atlas</a><nav class="topnav"><a href="../explorer">Explore</a><a href="../value-chains">Value chains</a><a href="../analysis">Research</a><a href="../method">Method</a><a href="../data">Download data</a></nav></div></header>
<main id="main">
<section class="hero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Solar photovoltaics · Manufacturing</div>
  <h1>$title</h1>
  <p class="deck">$deck</p>
  <p class="byline">Evidence review · <time datetime="$version">24 November 2026</time> · <a href="PRODUCTION.md">Claim register</a></p>
</div></section>
<article>
  <p><strong class="evidence-label">Calculated ceiling.</strong> Europe had about $module_capacity GW per year of solar module manufacturing capacity in the IEA’s 2025 estimates. It had much less wafer capacity. With compatible equipment, full availability and a route with no imports or stocks at those manufacturing stages, the reported stages could support at most $closed_ceiling GW per year of module-equivalent output, or about $ratio of module nameplate capacity. The IEA figures omit ingots, so enforcing that additional step could lower the ceiling.</p>
  <p>Capacity measures what factories are rated to make. Production measures what they actually make. The effect of a disruption also depends on qualified suppliers, inventories, utilization and how quickly alternative inputs can reach factories.</p>
  <p class="note"><a href="#wafer-sensitivity">Dated addendum on wafer sourcing and output</a></p>

  <h2>Europe’s upstream capacity is smaller</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="European solar manufacturing capacity">
    <table>
      <caption><strong class="evidence-label">Source estimates.</strong> Approximate 2025 manufacturing capacity in Europe as reported by the IEA. The comparison treats upstream GW as module-equivalent annual rates.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">GW-equivalent per year</th><th scope="col">Capacity on a common scale</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
$capacity_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>$europe. The commentary gives rounded figures for Europe. Its public materials do not specify country membership, conversion factors for the upstream rates, the share of operating lines, or technical compatibility.</p>
  <p><b>A separate EU-27 inventory.</b> In its August 2025 inventory, Fraunhofer lists no active ingot or wafer production in the EU-27 and identifies 0.4 GWp per year of ingot capacity closed in 2024–25. Its operating estimates for cells and modules were 2.9 and 6.7 GWp per year. Under that inventory, a route using only active EU-27 lines would need another source of ingots and wafers before it could operate. The narrower inventory cannot supply an ingot rate for the IEA’s wider Europe series. $eu.</p>
  <p><b>Inverters.</b> The IEA puts Europe’s inverter manufacturing capacity at about $inverter GW per year in 2025 across solar PV and battery applications. An inverter converts electricity after installation. It sits outside the serial module manufacturing calculation. $europe.</p>

  <h2>China’s share depends on the measure</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="China production and capacity shares">
    <table>
      <caption><strong class="evidence-label">Source estimates.</strong> China’s reported shares of global output and manufacturing capacity from separate sources</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">2023 output · IEA PVPS 2024</th><th scope="col">2024 capacity · IEA policy report 2026</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
$share_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The former 85–98% headline came from the four rounded 2023 output readings. The polysilicon output figure includes semiconductor grades. The 2023 module share includes thin film, and the adjacent reported volumes do not reproduce the chart’s 84.6%. The 2024 policy chart reports capacity shares on its own basis. $pvps and $cap_share.</p>
  <p>The IEA’s <em>Energy Technology Perspectives 2026</em> cites about 95% for wafers in its own 2024 capacity discussion. The July 2026 commentary repeats about 95% in its 2025 discussion and calls the wafer figure production, with no wafer-specific denominator. Differences in definitions and source vintages prevent a like-for-like trend. $etp and $europe.</p>

  <h2>Silicon use fell per watt of cell output</h2>
  <table>
    <caption><strong class="evidence-label">Source estimates.</strong> Rounded figures from the July 2026 Fraunhofer report</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Grams of polysilicon input per watt-peak of cell output</th><th scope="col">Wafer thickness, µm</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
$intensity_rows
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The earlier intensity was about $intensity_ratio times the later one. This compares manufacturing input with rated cell output. The estimate reflects thinner wafers, improved cell efficiency, reduced sawing losses and changes in recycling. The chart changes sources and includes recycling from 2017, so it cannot isolate how much each mechanism contributed. Grams per panel would also depend on the panel’s rated output and construction. $fraun.</p>

  <h2>A wafer supply stress test</h2>
  <p class="note">Figures in this section: <a href="../how-it-works#evidence" title="Modelled: depends on a model with assumptions or settings" style="display:inline-block;font-size:.66rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:2px 7px;border-radius:4px;vertical-align:.12em;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap;background:#efe9f6;color:#5b3a85">modelled</a> The import rate, exposure, yields and stocks are assumptions, not observed data.</p>
  <p><strong>Illustrative scenarios.</strong> The model follows one compatible crystalline-silicon route through polysilicon, ingots, wafers, cells and modules. The six-month open case assumes wafer deliveries sufficient to fill cell capacity at the stated point assumptions. The shocks remove half or all of the assumed China-origin wafer lane. The model varies that lane’s exposure from zero to all imports and usable ingot capacity from zero to the rate needed to support the published wafer capacity. No observed sourcing or inventory data set these values.</p>
  <section id="wafer-sensitivity" aria-labelledby="wafer-sensitivity-title">
    <h3 id="wafer-sensitivity-title">How sourcing changes the result</h3>
    <p class="note">Addendum · <time datetime="$review_date">24 November 2026</time></p>
    <p>The shock removes the exposed China-origin share of an assumed wafer import rate of $normal_wafer_rate GW-equivalent per year. The domestic wafer nameplate rate is fixed at $fixed_wafer_rate GW-equivalent per year. Rows vary the compatible ingot supply those plants can use. Columns vary the share of the assumed import lane lost. The model assumes no replacement shipments or opening stocks.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Module output under a wafer supply shock">
      <table class="sensitivity-grid">
        <caption><strong class="evidence-label">Modelled.</strong> Module-equivalent output in GW over $months months. Darker cells show higher output.</caption>
        <colgroup><col></colgroup>
        <colgroup span="5"></colgroup>
        <thead>
          <tr><td></td><th colspan="5" scope="colgroup">China-origin share of assumed wafer imports lost</th></tr>
          <tr><th scope="col">Usable ingots (GW-equivalent/year)</th>$sensitivity_header</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
$sensitivity_rows
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>At the highest assumed ingot rate, module output reaches $output_with_imports GW over $months months with the import lane intact and falls to $output_without_imports GW if the whole lane is lost. The wider Europe test has no plant-level sourcing data to establish which part of the table describes current supply.</p>

    <h4>What the EU-27 inventory can bound</h4>
    <p><strong class="evidence-label">Modelled from an operating-capacity inventory.</strong> In its August 2025 EU-27 inventory, Fraunhofer lists no active ingot or wafer plants. With no stocks or other imported intermediates, the compatible route yields $no_wafer_output GW over $months months without wafer deliveries. Supplying qualified wafers at an annual rate of $full_wafer_rate GW-equivalent would support up to $full_wafer_output GW in the same period, assuming full availability, compatibility and unit yields. This example uses the EU-27 inventory alone. The delivery rate is hypothetical. $eu.</p>

    <h4>What a supply agreement can establish</h4>
    <p>In 2022, Meyer Burger announced a binding agreement for wafers from Norwegian Crystals and reported initial deliveries for its heterojunction production route. Norway sits outside the EU-27. The company gave no delivery volume, and IEA PVPS records that Norwegian Crystals ceased operations in 2023. In Spain, Sunwafe described an integrated ingot and wafer project with commercial operations targeted for 2029. These examples distinguish a historical supply lane from proposed capacity. Estimating replacement supply today requires evidence of operating lines, qualified buyers and available shipments. <a href="https://www.semiconductorpackagingnews.com/uploads/1/20220816_MBTN_MM_Wafer_EN.pdf">Meyer Burger release</a>, <a href="https://www.iea-pvps.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/PVPS_Annual_Report_2023_v4-6.pdf#page=91">IEA PVPS Norway report</a> and <a href="https://sunwafe.com/sunwafe-secures-site-approval-and-appoints-ceo-to-advance-europes-first-large-scale-solar-wafer-plant/">Sunwafe announcement</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Solar wafer supply scenario results">
    <table>
      <caption><strong class="evidence-label">Modelled.</strong> Module output in GW-equivalent over $months months. Ranges are from assumed inputs.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Scenario</th><th scope="col">Point output</th><th scope="col">Output range</th><th scope="col">Loss range versus matched open case</th><th scope="col">Active capacity limits at the point</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
$scenario_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>At the severe case’s point assumptions, adding capacity at either the ingot or wafer stage alone leaves the other stage limiting supply. Adding one GW per year at both raises output by $paired_gain GW over the six-month shock. Imports of qualified cells could bypass the wafer and cell stages. This stress test holds that route outside the calculation so it isolates exposure to the wafer lane.</p>

  <h2>Comparing ways to preserve output</h2>
  <p class="note">Figures in this section: <a href="../how-it-works#evidence" title="Modelled: depends on a model with assumptions or settings" style="display:inline-block;font-size:.66rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:2px 7px;border-radius:4px;vertical-align:.12em;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap;background:#efe9f6;color:#5b3a85">modelled</a> The same assumed model as the stress test above.</p>
  <p>In the severe case, preserving $target_fraction of the open point output means producing $target_gw GW over the shock. The model requires $wafer_needed GW-equivalent of additional compatible wafers. This puts manufacturing, qualified imports and inventories on the same physical target.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Integrated production.</b> Add $wafer_rate GW-equivalent per year of wafer throughput and the ingot supply to support it before the shock.</li>
    <li><b>Diversified sourcing.</b> Arrange for suppliers outside the affected lane to deliver $wafer_needed GW-equivalent of qualified wafers during the shock.</li>
    <li><b>Stocks.</b> Hold $wafer_needed GW-equivalent of compatible wafers available at the start. The reserve is used once and must be replenished.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The IEA estimates that EU production with North African wafers could cost almost 20% less than fully EU-made modules under its same-size facility assumptions. A cost ranking at equal resilience would also need qualified capacity, delivery terms, inventory carrying costs and the length and frequency of interruptions. $cost.</p>
  <p>See the <a href="README.md">methods and reproduction instructions</a>, <a href="evidence_inputs.json">source data and scenario inputs</a>, and <a href="out/solar_chain.json">calculated evidence</a>.</p>

  <h2>What the customs data cover</h2>
  <p>The table shows nominal export values and leading exporters from BACI. HS 280461 includes multiple grades of high-purity silicon. HS 854140 in the historical nomenclatures includes cells, modules and other photosensitive devices. These categories cannot establish direct wafer sourcing or trace wafers embedded in traded modules.</p>
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="control" id="static-trade" hidden>
      <label for="trade-slider">Trade year</label><input id="trade-slider" type="range" min="0" max="0" value="0">
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="World exports in broad solar-related categories">
      <table>
        <caption><strong class="evidence-label">Customs data.</strong> CEPII BACI V202601, <span id="trade-year" aria-live="polite">$trade_year</span></caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Traded category</th><th scope="col">Nominal export value</th><th scope="col">Leading exporter</th><th scope="col">Scope</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody id="trade-body">
$trade_rows
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <a href="out/solar_trade.json">Download the yearly trade data</a>
  </div>

  <h2>Sources and evidence gaps</h2>
  <p>The <a href="PRODUCTION.md">claim register</a> records source dates and definitions. Estimating actual disruption losses would require matched plant data, qualified import flows, inventories and yields by wafer and cell technology. The wafer figures, cost comparison and hypothetical shocks answer different questions.</p>
